<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    Video to GIF: an animated GIF of a part of a video, made by the ffmpeg that comes with the program. Two runs, as ffmpeg's own guide for good GIFs says: the first finds the best palette of
    256 colours (or fewer) for the frames that are used, the second draws the frames with it (a GIF has one palette, so one made for a video of a screen looks far better than the default one).
    Also what the front end needs to ask: the range of frames (all of the video unless told), the size the GIF will have, how many frames it will have, and a reader that tells what is in a GIF
    that was made (its size, frames, length and repeats).

    No user interface, nothing of Windows and no other project of the solution: the video arrives as a path and a few numbers (PhotoLikerUI reads them with PhotoLiker.Video), what leaves
    is a file. The messages of an error are English; the front end words them for the person (see GifException.Reason).
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <RootNamespace>PhotoLiker.VideoToGif</RootNamespace>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <AssemblyAttribute Include="System.Runtime.CompilerServices.InternalsVisibleTo">
      <_Parameter1>PhotoLiker.VideoToGif.Tests</_Parameter1>
    </AssemblyAttribute>
    <AssemblyAttribute Include="System.Runtime.CompilerServices.InternalsVisibleTo">
      <_Parameter1>PhotoLikerUI.Tests</_Parameter1>
    </AssemblyAttribute>
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
